    description: { en: "Learn about nishin, the herring behind kazunoko, its historic role in Hokkaido's economy, and its traditional preparations.", ja: "数の子の親魚であるニシンと、北海道経済における歴史的役割、そして伝統的な調理法について学びます。", vi: "Tim hieu ve nishin, loai ca trich la nguon goc cua kazunoko, vai tro lich su cua no trong kinh te Hokkaido, va cac cach che bien truyen thong." },
    goal: { en: "After completing this mission, you will understand what nishin is as a fish, its historic importance to Hokkaido, and how it is traditionally prepared and preserved.", ja: "このミッションを終えると、魚としてのニシン、北海道における歴史的重要性、そして伝統的な調理・保存方法を理解できます。", vi: "Sau khi hoan thanh nhiem vu nay, ban se hieu nishin la loai ca nhu the nao, tam quan trong lich su cua no doi voi Hokkaido, va cach no duoc che bien va bao quan theo truyen thong." },
    content: {
      en: `Nishin is Pacific herring, the adult fish behind the prized New Year's roe kazunoko covered in an earlier mission — but the fish itself, apart from its eggs, has its own substantial place in Japanese food history. Nishin is an oily, silver-skinned fish that once ran in enormous schools along the coast of Hokkaido, and for much of the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries it was one of the most economically important fisheries in Japan.

That economic importance is hard to overstate. During the height of the Hokkaido herring boom, catches were so large that herring was processed not only as food but as fertilizer for agriculture elsewhere in Japan, and the wealth generated by the fishery built entire coastal communities. Some of the grand wooden merchant houses from that era, known as nishin goten ("herring palaces"), still stand today as physical evidence of how central this single fish once was to the regional economy, even though herring stocks later collapsed and never fully recovered to their historic scale.

Because nishin was caught in such volume before refrigeration existed, preservation techniques developed around it were essential rather than optional. Drying and smoking were the primary methods: migaki-nishin, dried and filleted herring, became a shelf-stable staple that could be reconstituted and cooked long after the fishing season ended. This dried herring became so embedded in regional cooking that it gave rise to nishin soba, a Kyoto specialty in which a piece of simmered, sweetened dried herring is served on top of soba noodles, a dish that has nothing to do with sushi but everything to do with how far nishin's culinary reach extends beyond the coast where it was caught.

The most iconic home-style preparation of the fish itself is konbu-maki, in which a fillet of herring is rolled up inside a sheet of kombu kelp and simmered slowly in a sweetened soy-based broth until both the fish and the kelp turn tender. Konbu-maki is a staple of osechi ryori, the New Year's feast, where it sits alongside kazunoko as another herring-derived good-luck dish, though its symbolism rests more on the kelp itself, whose name kobu puns with the word for joy, than on the herring inside it.

At sushi counters specifically, nishin is a much rarer sight than fish like kohada or aji, but it does appear at some traditional shops in a cured or lightly vinegared form, shime-nishin, prepared in a manner conceptually similar to how other oily, silver-skinned fish are cured before serving. Its presence on a menu is generally a sign of a shop leaning into older, regional Japanese cooking traditions rather than a routine everyday offering.`,
      ja: `ニシンは数の子を生む親魚であり、それ自体も日本の食文化において重要な位置を占めてきた。かつて北海道沿岸で大群をなして獲れ、19世紀から20世紀初頭にかけて食用にも肥料にも用いられる一大産業となり、その繁栄は「鰊御殿」と呼ばれる豪商の屋敷跡に今も見て取れる。冷蔵技術がなかった時代には乾燥や燻製による保存が発達し、身欠きニシンは京都の「にしんそば」など各地の郷土料理にも受け継がれている。家庭料理としては昆布で巻いて甘辛く煮た「昆布巻き」が代表的で、御節料理にも欠かせない一品となっている。寿司店で扱われることは少ないが、酢締めにした「〆ニシン」として伝統的な店に登場することもある。`,
      vi: `Nishin la ca trich Thai Binh Duong, ca me cho ra trung kazunoko, va ban than no cung co vi tri quan trong trong lich su am thuc Nhat Ban. Tung boi thanh dan lon doc bo bien Hokkaido, nishin la nganh danh bat kinh te quan trong bac nhat vao the ky 19 va dau the ky 20, duoc dung lam thuc pham lan phan bon, va su thinh vuong do de lai dau an qua nhung ngoi nha thuong gia go lon goi la "nishin goten" (cung dien ca trich). Truoc khi co tu lanh, ca duoc bao quan bang cach phoi kho va xong khoi, tao ra "migaki-nishin" — nguyen lieu cho mon "nishin soba" noi tieng cua Kyoto. Mon che bien tai gia tieu bieu nhat la "konbu-maki" — ca trich cuon trong rong bien kombu va ninh trong nuoc tuong ngot, mot mon khong the thieu trong osechi ryori. Tai quay sushi, nishin hiem gap hon so voi kohada hay aji, nhung van xuat hien duoi dang uop giam "shime-nishin" o mot so quan truyen thong.`,
    },
    culturalInsight: { en: "The nishin goten merchant houses that still stand along Hokkaido's coast are a physical reminder of how a single fish once shaped an entire regional economy, long before it was known mainly for its roe.", ja: "北海道沿岸に今も残る鰊御殿は、一種類の魚がかつて地域経済全体を形作っていたことを物語る貴重な証であり、それは数の子として知られるずっと以前の話である。", vi: "Nhung ngoi nha thuong gia nishin goten con ton tai doc bo bien Hokkaido la minh chung vat chat cho viec mot loai ca duy nhat tung dinh hinh ca mot nen kinh te vung, tu lau truoc khi no duoc biet den chu yeu qua trung cua no." },
    chefTip: { en: "If you encounter nishin soba or konbu-maki, taste them as a way of understanding herring on its own terms, separate from kazunoko, since most people meet this fish only through its roe.", ja: "にしんそばや昆布巻きに出会ったら、それを数の子とは切り離してニシンそのものを理解する機会と捉えるとよい。多くの人はこの魚を卵を通してしか知らないからである。", vi: "Neu ban gap mon nishin soba hay konbu-maki, hay nem thu de hieu ve ca trich theo dung ban chat cua no, tach biet khoi kazunoko, vi phan lon moi nguoi chi biet den loai ca nay qua trung cua no." },
    keyTakeaway: { en: "Nishin built a regional economy long before its roe made it famous, and it still lives on in dried, smoked, and simmered forms far beyond the sushi counter.", ja: "ニシンはその卵で有名になるずっと前から地域経済を築いており、乾燥・燻製・煮物といった形で今も寿司店の外で生き続けている。", vi: "Nishin da xay dung nen mot nen kinh te vung tu lau truoc khi trung cua no noi tieng, va no van ton tai duoi dang phoi kho, xong khoi va ham, xa vuot ra ngoai quay sushi." },
