    difficulty: "beginner",
    primarySkill: "fish_knowledge",
    description: { en: "Learn about amaebi, the raw sweet shrimp whose sugary flavor develops after catching, and how its head and tail are both put to use.", ja: "捕獲後に甘みが増していく生の甘海老と、頭と尾の両方を余すことなく活かす調理法について学びます。", vi: "Tim hieu ve amaebi, loai tom ngot an song ma vi ngot phat trien sau khi danh bat, va cach ca dau lan than tom deu duoc su dung tron ven." },
    goal: { en: "After completing this mission, you will understand what amaebi is, why it is served raw unlike boiled ebi, and where its distinctive sweetness comes from.", ja: "このミッションを終えると、甘海老とは何か、なぜボイルされる海老とは違い生で提供されるのか、そしてその独特な甘みがどこから来るのかを理解できます。", vi: "Sau khi hoan thanh nhiem vu nay, ban se hieu amaebi la gi, tai sao no duoc phuc vu song khac voi ebi luoc, va vi ngot dac trung cua no den tu dau." },
    content: {
      en: `Amaebi, "sweet shrimp," refers most often to northern shrimp or pink shrimp caught in the cold waters off northern Japan, and it stands in direct contrast to the ebi covered in an earlier mission. Where that shrimp is typically boiled before being served as nigiri, amaebi is served raw, its translucent pinkish-orange flesh glistening on the rice with none of the cooking that firms and opaques ebi's flesh. This raw presentation is central to amaebi's identity — it is one of the relatively few shrimp preparations in sushi where the shrimp is not cooked at all before serving.

The sweetness that gives amaebi its name is not simply a matter of the shrimp's natural flavor at the moment of catching. Like a number of other seafoods, amaebi's flesh continues to change chemically after death, as enzymes break down proteins and glycogen stored in the muscle into simpler sugars and amino acids. This means amaebi often tastes noticeably sweeter after resting for a short period, sometimes a day or more, than it does if eaten the instant it is caught. It is a clear example of a broader principle in seafood handling: for certain ingredients, a controlled rest period improves flavor rather than diminishing it, and "fresher is always better" does not hold universally.

Amaebi is also a showcase of whole-ingredient use. The head, which contains rich, flavorful fat and roe when present, is very commonly removed and deep-fried separately, served alongside the raw tail meat as its own small dish. This transforms what might otherwise be discarded into a crisp, savory, umami-dense counterpart to the tender, sweet raw meat — a shell that shatters with a satisfying crunch and releases a concentrated shrimp flavor quite different from the mild sweetness of the tail.

Together, the raw tail and the fried head give amaebi a built-in contrast within a single order: soft versus crisp, delicately sweet versus richly savory. That contrast, along with the specific enzymatic sweetness that develops with time, is what distinguishes amaebi from other shrimp preparations in the sushi repertoire and is part of why it remains a distinctive, memorable order rather than simply "raw shrimp."`,
      ja: `甘海老は北日本近海の冷たい海で獲れるホッコクアカエビなどを指し、加熱するボイル海老とは対照的に生のまま握られる。名前の由来となる甘さは、捕獲直後よりもむしろ、酵素の働きによって身のタンパク質やグリコーゲンが糖やアミノ酸へと分解される時間の経過とともに増していくもので、少し寝かせたほうが甘みが際立つことが多い。頭は取り除いて別に唐揚げにし、生の尾の身と共に提供することが一般的で、余すところなく食材を活かす代表的な例となっている。柔らかく甘い尾の身とパリッとした濃厚な頭の唐揚げという対比が、甘海老を単なる「生海老」以上の一皿にしている。`,
      vi: `Amaebi thuong la tom hong hoac tom phuong bac danh bat o vung bien lanh phia bac Nhat Ban, va no doi lap ro ret voi ebi luoc chin. Amaebi duoc phuc vu song, thit trong hong cam long lanh tren com, khong qua che bien nhiet. Vi ngot lam nen ten goi cua no khong don gian den tu luc vua danh bat, ma phat trien qua thoi gian khi enzyme phan giai protein va glycogen trong thit thanh duong va axit amin don gian hon — vi vay amaebi thuong ngot hon ro ret sau khi nghi mot thoi gian ngan, dam bao rang "tuoi hon luon tot hon" khong phai luc nao cung dung. Phan dau tom, giau chat beo va dam da, thuong duoc chien gion rieng va phuc vu cung voi phan than song, tao nen su tuong phan giua vi ngot diu cua than tom va vi dam da gion tan cua dau tom chien — mot vi du dien hinh cho viec tan dung tron ven nguyen lieu.`,
    },
    culturalInsight: { en: "Amaebi is a striking demonstration that raw seafood is not always best eaten instantly — its signature sweetness is a product of enzymatic change over time, not a snapshot of the moment of catching.", ja: "甘海老は、生の海鮮が必ずしも捕獲直後が最良とは限らないことを示す好例であり、その甘さは捕獲の瞬間ではなく時間をかけた酵素変化の産物である。", vi: "Amaebi la minh chung noi bat rang hai san song khong phai luc nao cung ngon nhat khi an ngay lap tuc — vi ngot dac trung cua no la san pham cua su thay doi enzyme theo thoi gian, khong phai khoanh khac vua danh bat." },
    chefTip: { en: "Do not rush amaebi straight from boat to plate — allow it a short, controlled rest period, since its signature sweetness develops through enzymatic breakdown after catching, not at the moment of capture.", ja: "甘海老を獲れたてのまますぐに提供しようとしないこと。その特徴的な甘さは捕獲後の酵素分解によって生まれるため、短時間でも管理された熟成期間を設けるとよい。", vi: "Dung voi vang phuc vu amaebi ngay khi vua danh bat — hay de no nghi mot khoang thoi gian ngan co kiem soat, vi vi ngot dac trung cua no phat trien qua qua trinh phan giai enzyme sau khi bat, khong phai o khoanh khac vua bat duoc." },
    keyTakeaway: { en: "Amaebi's sweetness is earned through time, not just freshness, and its fried head alongside its raw tail proves that nothing on the shrimp needs to go to waste.", ja: "甘海老の甘さは鮮度だけでなく時間によっても培われるものであり、揚げた頭と生の尾を共に出すことで、海老には無駄にするところが何もないことを証明している。", vi: "Vi ngot cua amaebi duoc tao ra qua thoi gian, khong chi don thuan qua do tuoi, va viec dau chien di kem than song chung minh rang khong co phan nao cua con tom can bi bo phi." },
